\title{Thermal conductivity of boron nitride–epoxy composites}
\author{A. Author¹, B. Coauthor¹, C. Reviewer²\footnote{Example document (single-column layout). All names, institutions, data and references are fictional.}\thanks{a.author@example.org} \\
{\small\itshape ¹ Department of Materials Engineering, Northfield University} \\
{\small\itshape ² Halden Institute of Applied Physics}}
\begin{document}
\maketitle





\begin{abstract}
Epoxy resins are good electrical insulators but poor conductors of heat, which limits their use in power electronics. We disperse hexagonal boron nitride platelets in a bisphenol-A epoxy at volume fractions from 0 to 30\% and measure the thermal conductivity at 25 °C with a transient hot-wire probe. The conductivity rises from 0.20 W/(m K) for the neat resin to 0.44 W/(m K) at 30 vol\%, a factor of 2.2. A Maxwell effective-medium model describes all compositions with a single filler conductivity of about 10 W/(m K), far below the bulk value, pointing to interfacial resistance as the limiting factor.
\end{abstract}

\begin{keyword}
thermal conductivity \sep epoxy composites \sep boron nitride \sep effective-medium theory
\end{keyword}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Heat removal is a central constraint in compact power electronics, where a polymer encapsulant must insulate electrically yet carry heat away from the device. Neat epoxies conduct only about 0.2 W/(m K), two to three orders of magnitude less than ceramics. The usual remedy is to load the resin with a ceramic filler, and hexagonal boron nitride (h-BN) is attractive because it combines a high in-plane conductivity with electrical resistivity above 10\textsuperscript{12} Ω cm \cite{lindqvist2019,okafor2021}.

Reported gains vary widely between laboratories, partly because the measurement method, the platelet size and the filler–matrix interface differ from study to study. Here we prepare a single series of composites from one resin batch and one filler grade, measure them with one method, and test whether a simple effective-medium model can describe the whole series. Section \ref{sec:methods} describes the samples and the measurement, Section \ref{sec:results} gives the results, and Section \ref{sec:discussion} discusses them.

\section{Materials and methods}
\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Sample preparation}

Composites were made in five steps:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The filler (h-BN platelets, median lateral size 5 µm) was dried for 12 h at 120 °C.
  \item The resin and hardener were mixed at the stoichiometric ratio and degassed for 10 min.
  \item The filler was added in portions and homogenised in a planetary mixer at 2000 rpm.
  \item The mixture was cast into cylindrical moulds 40 mm in diameter and 80 mm tall.
  \item Samples were cured for 2 h at 80 °C and 4 h at 120 °C.
\end{enumerate}

Six compositions were prepared, from 0 to 30 vol\% filler. The densities of the resin and the filler are 1.15 and 2.10 g/cm³, and the density of each composite follows from the rule of mixtures (Table \ref{tab:results}).

\subsection{Thermal measurements}

The thermal conductivity was measured at 25 °C with a transient hot-wire probe embedded in each cylinder. A constant heating power $q$ per unit length is applied to the wire and the temperature rise $\Delta T$ is recorded against time $t$. For an ideal line source in an infinite medium the conductivity follows from the slope of $\Delta T$ against $\ln t$:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hotwire}
k = \frac{q}{4\pi}\,\left(\frac{\mathrm{d}\,\Delta T}{\mathrm{d}\ln t}\right)^{-1}.
\end{equation}

Each value is the mean of five runs separated by 15 minutes to let the sample relax\footnote{Only the window between 1 s and 10 s was fitted, to avoid wire-contact effects at early times and boundary effects at late times.}; the uncertainty is the larger of the run-to-run scatter and the 3\% calibration uncertainty of the probe.

\subsection{Effective-medium model}

We compare the data with the Maxwell model for spherical inclusions of conductivity $k_f$ at volume fraction $\phi$ in a matrix of conductivity $k_m$:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:maxwell}
k_{\mathrm{eff}} = k_m\,\frac{k_f + 2k_m + 2\phi\,(k_f - k_m)}{k_f + 2k_m - \phi\,(k_f - k_m)}.
\end{equation}

With $k_m$ fixed at the measured value of the neat resin, $k_f$ is the only free parameter. It is found by minimising

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:chi2}
\chi^2(k_f) = \sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(\frac{k_i - k_{\mathrm{eff}}(\phi_i; k_f)}{\sigma_i}\right)^2,
\end{equation}

over the $N = 5$ filled samples, where $k_i$ and $\sigma_i$ are the measured conductivity and its uncertainty.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Table \ref{tab:results} lists the measured conductivities. The neat resin gives $k_m = 0.20\ \mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, and the conductivity grows monotonically with filler content to $0.44\ \mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ at 30 vol\%.

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{rrrr}
    \hline
    \textbf{Frac.} & \textbf{Meas.} & \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Gain} \\ \hline
    0.00 & 0.20 & 0.20 & 1.00 \\
    0.05 & 0.23 & 0.23 & 1.15 \\
    0.10 & 0.27 & 0.26 & 1.35 \\
    0.15 & 0.29 & 0.30 & 1.45 \\
    0.20 & 0.34 & 0.34 & 1.70 \\
    0.30 & 0.44 & 0.44 & 2.20 \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Filler volume fraction (Frac.), measured and modelled thermal conductivity k (W/(m K)) at 25 °C, and enhancement over the neat resin. Uncertainties: 0.010 to 0.020 W/(m K). The model uses a filler conductivity of 9.7 W/(m K).}
  \label{tab:results}
\end{table}

Minimising Eq. \eqref{eq:chi2} gives $k_f = 9.7\ \mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ with $\chi^2 = 1.4$ for 4 degrees of freedom. The one-standard-deviation interval, 5.7 to 28 W/(m K), is asymmetric because the model saturates when $k_f \gg k_m$. All model values in Table \ref{tab:results} lie within one standard deviation of the measurements.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The fitted filler conductivity is much lower than the in-plane value of several hundred W/(m K) reported for single h-BN crystals \cite{lindqvist2019}. This is expected for randomly oriented platelets separated from the matrix by an interfacial thermal resistance, and it is consistent with other epoxy–ceramic composites \cite{okafor2021,tanaka2023}. Because Eq. \eqref{eq:maxwell} is insensitive to $k_f$ above about 30 W/(m K), the data cannot tell a poor interface from a poor filler; direct interface or single-platelet measurements would be needed.

Two limitations should be kept in mind. The Maxwell model ignores platelet shape and orientation, so it cannot describe the onset of percolation expected above 30 vol\%. And all samples came from one resin batch, so batch-to-batch variation is not included in the uncertainties.

\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclusions}

Adding 30 vol\% h-BN raises the thermal conductivity of an epoxy resin from 0.20 to 0.44 W/(m K). A one-parameter Maxwell model fits the whole series with an effective filler conductivity of about 10 W/(m K), which suggests that improving the filler–matrix interface, rather than the filler itself, is the most promising route to higher conductivity.

\section*{Acknowledgements}

This work was supported by the Example Foundation (grant EF-0000). We thank the technical staff of the Northfield materials workshop for help with the moulds, and the maintainers of \href{https://tectonic-typesetting.github.io/}{Tectonic}, the LaTeX engine used to typeset this document.

