    Compile now & Ctrl+Shift+C & Compile \\
    New comment & Ctrl+Alt+M & Review \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Keyboard shortcuts used in this tour.}
  \label{tab:shortcuts}
\end{table}

\section{Figures, links, footnotes and citations}

Figure on the Insert tab places an image with a caption; relative paths are resolved against the document's folder, and a missing image shows as a labelled placeholder instead of breaking the build. Drawing and the Flowchart builder (Ctrl+Shift+F), in the same group, draw simple diagrams without leaving the editor.

Hyperlinks (Ctrl+K) look like \href{https://tectonic-typesetting.github.io/}{the Tectonic project}, the LaTeX engine that compiles your documents. A footnote\footnote{A footnote is typed inline and printed at the foot of the page in the PDF.} is typed in place and moves to the bottom of the page in the PDF. A citation such as \cite{example2026} points to an entry of the reference list at the end of this document; Citation on the References tab lists the keys available.

\section{Layout: pages and columns}

The Layout tab sets the margins, the page size and one, two or three columns, and reflows everything at once. Document type switches between article, report, book, the journal classes and the rest; the page you type on takes the fonts, headings and spacing of the PDF that type prints. File > Document properties holds the font, font size and line spacing, and Insert > Multi-column region puts a two-column block inside a single-column page.

\section{Versions with Git}

Each saved document lives in a Git repository. On the History tab, \textbf{Save snapshot and upload} records a version, and \textbf{View version history} lets you browse every snapshot and go back to any of them. Snapshots use your normal Git identity, so they look the same as commits made from the command line.

\section{Review: comments, highlights and Overleaf}

Select a passage and press New comment on the Review tab (Ctrl+Alt+M) to leave a note for yourself or a co-author; Accept and Reject sit beside it. Highlight, on the Home tab, marks text without changing it.

The Share group on the Review tab works with co-authors on Overleaf: \textbf{Send to Overleaf} puts your version into an Overleaf project and \textbf{Get changes from Overleaf} brings theirs back. Both need an Overleaf plan with Git access; on a free plan, \textbf{Download for Overleaf} makes a zip to upload as a new project.

\section{Importing and exporting}

File > Import reads existing \texttt{.tex}, Word (.docx), PDF and Markdown files. Anything the editor does not understand is kept as a raw LaTeX block, so an imported file can be exported again without losing content. File > Export writes .tex, .docx or .pdf.

Tools > Compiler chooses between the LaTeX engine and Typst, and \textbf{Compiler status} tells you whether the engine and its offline package cache are ready. Everything compiles offline, straight after installation.

\section{Where to go next}

Open the \textbf{Examples} tab for starting points: articles in one, two or three columns, journal papers, theses and books, lab reports, letters, a CV, slides, posters and planners. Each opens in its own window so this tour stays open. The Help tab has the user guide (F1) and every keyboard shortcut.

Happy writing. \textit{The KherveTeX team}

\begin{thebibliography}{99}
\bibitem{example2026} I.~Writer and J.~Typesetter, \emph{Writing documents visually: an example reference}, Example Press, 2026.
\end{thebibliography}
\end{document}

