  \item Layout: margins, page size, one to three columns and the document type (article, thesis, journal class and so on).
  \item Examples: ready-made documents of every type, this tour, and your own templates.
  \item References: citations, footnotes, captions and cross-references.
  \item Review: spelling, comments, and sharing with Overleaf.
  \item Compile: compile now, auto-compile, and PDF output.
  \item History: snapshots and the version history.
  \item View: the page, the LaTeX code, the console and the PDF.
  \item Help: the user guide, keyboard shortcuts and updates.
\end{itemize}

Click in a table, a picture or an equation and extra tabs (Table Design, Table Layout, Picture, Theorem, Equation) appear beside the others, as in Word. The small buttons on the left of the tab names save, open, print and undo; Compile PDF sits on the right. The menu bar above holds everything else (File, Edit, View, Insert, Format, Tools, Window, Help), and the switch at the bottom right of the window flips between the light and dark themes.

\section{Quick start}

The shortest path from an empty page to a finished PDF:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Press Ctrl+N (Cmd+N on a Mac) for a blank page, or pick a ready-made document from the Examples tab.
  \item Choose the document type on the Layout tab, then type your title and text. The Styles gallery on the Home tab turns a paragraph into a heading, an abstract or a keyword line.
  \item Add what you need from the Insert and References tabs: a table, an equation, a footnote, a citation, a cross-reference.
  \item Watch the PDF beside the page (Ctrl+4), or press Compile PDF at the top right (Ctrl+Shift+C).
  \item Save with Ctrl+S. Export a PDF, a .tex file or a Word file from File > Export.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Views: page, code and PDF}

The View tab shows the same document three ways. \textbf{Visual} (Ctrl+1) is this page, where you write. \textbf{Code} (Ctrl+2) shows the LaTeX that KherveTeX generates, so you can read it, copy it or learn from it. \textbf{PDF} keeps the compiled result beside the page (Ctrl+4), and \textbf{Detach PDF} moves it to its own window, for a second screen. Turn it off to write undisturbed.

If something looks wrong in the PDF, the \textbf{Console} button on the View tab lists the compiler messages in plain text.

\section{Formatting text}

Select text and use the Font group on the Home tab to make it \textbf{bold}, \textit{italic}, \underline{underlined}, \sout{struck through} or \texttt{monospaced}. Subscripts (H\textsubscript{2}O), superscripts (m\textsuperscript{2}) and \textsc{small caps} work the same way. Every mark becomes the matching LaTeX command, so the PDF always agrees with the page.

\subsection{Headings and paragraph styles}

The Styles gallery on the Home tab turns the current paragraph into a heading (five levels, numbered automatically), a title, an author line, an abstract or a keyword line. Each tile is drawn in the font and size the document type prints it in. Alignment (left, centre, right, justified) sits in the Paragraph group beside it.

\subsection{Lists}

The list buttons in the Paragraph group start a bulleted or numbered list; items nest with Tab and Shift+Tab. Inside an item you can use bold, links, footnotes and inline math like any other text. Some things worth knowing:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Use a bulleted list for things with no order, such as an inventory or a set of options.
  \item Use a numbered list for a procedure, where the order matters.
  \item Nested lists change their bullet style on their own at each depth.
\end{itemize}

\section{Mathematics}

The Equations group on the Insert tab holds it all. Inline math such as $E = mc^2$ is Ctrl+M; a displayed equation is Ctrl+Shift+M. The Equation builder (Ctrl+Shift+E) offers fractions, integrals, matrices and more as templates, and the Symbol palette (Ctrl+Shift+G) holds Greek letters, operators and arrows. A numbered, labelled equation:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:gauss}
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-x^{2}}\,dx = \sqrt{\pi}
\end{equation}

Multi-line derivations use the aligned environments. Here is the quadratic formula derived by completing the square:

\begin{align}
  a x^2 + b x + c &= 0 \\
  \left(x + \tfrac{b}{2a}\right)^2 &= \tfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2} \\
  x &= \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}
\end{align}

To point back at an equation, use Cross-reference on the References tab and pick it from the list: Equation \eqref{eq:gauss} is the Gaussian integral above. If the equation is renumbered later, the reference follows.

\section{Tables}

Table on the Insert tab creates a grid you fill in directly, with a caption and a label for cross-references. While the caret is in a table, the Table Design and Table Layout tabs add rows, columns, styles and shading. Table \ref{tab:shortcuts} collects the shortcuts used in this tour (on a Mac, read Ctrl as Cmd).

\begin{table}[H]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{lll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Action} & \textbf{Shortcut} & \textbf{Ribbon tab} \\ \hline
    Bold / italic / underline & Ctrl+B / I / U & Home \\
    Inline math & Ctrl+M & Insert \\
    Math block & Ctrl+Shift+M & Insert \\
    Equation builder & Ctrl+Shift+E & Insert \\
    Symbol palette & Ctrl+Shift+G & Insert \\
    Hyperlink & Ctrl+K & Insert \\
    Visual / Code view & Ctrl+1 / 2 & View \\
    PDF beside the page & Ctrl+4 & View \\
    Compile now & Ctrl+Shift+C & Compile \\
    New comment & Ctrl+Alt+M & Review \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Keyboard shortcuts used in this tour.}
  \label{tab:shortcuts}
\end{table}

\section{Figures, links, footnotes and citations}

Figure on the Insert tab places an image with a caption; relative paths are resolved against the document's folder, and a missing image shows as a labelled placeholder instead of breaking the build. Drawing and the Flowchart builder (Ctrl+Shift+F), in the same group, draw simple diagrams without leaving the editor.

Hyperlinks (Ctrl+K) look like \href{https://tectonic-typesetting.github.io/}{the Tectonic project}, the LaTeX engine that compiles your documents. A footnote\footnote{A footnote is typed inline and printed at the foot of the page in the PDF.} is typed in place and moves to the bottom of the page in the PDF. A citation such as \cite{example2026} points to an entry of the reference list at the end of this document; Citation on the References tab lists the keys available.

\section{Layout: pages and columns}

The Layout tab sets the margins, the page size and one, two or three columns, and reflows everything at once. Document type switches between article, report, book, the journal classes and the rest; the page you type on takes the fonts, headings and spacing of the PDF that type prints. File > Document properties holds the font, font size and line spacing, and Insert > Multi-column region puts a two-column block inside a single-column page.

\section{Versions with Git}

Each saved document lives in a Git repository. On the History tab, \textbf{Save snapshot and upload} records a version, and \textbf{View version history} lets you browse every snapshot and go back to any of them. Snapshots use your normal Git identity, so they look the same as commits made from the command line.

\section{Review: comments, highlights and Overleaf}

Select a passage and press New comment on the Review tab (Ctrl+Alt+M) to leave a note for yourself or a co-author; Accept and Reject sit beside it. Highlight, on the Home tab, marks text without changing it.

The Share group on the Review tab works with co-authors on Overleaf: \textbf{Send to Overleaf} puts your version into an Overleaf project and \textbf{Get changes from Overleaf} brings theirs back. Both need an Overleaf plan with Git access; on a free plan, \textbf{Download for Overleaf} makes a zip to upload as a new project.

\section{Importing and exporting}

File > Import reads existing \texttt{.tex}, Word (.docx), PDF and Markdown files. Anything the editor does not understand is kept as a raw LaTeX block, so an imported file can be exported again without losing content. File > Export writes .tex, .docx or .pdf.

Tools > Compiler chooses between the LaTeX engine and Typst, and \textbf{Compiler status} tells you whether the engine and its offline package cache are ready. Everything compiles offline, straight after installation.

\section{Where to go next}

Open the \textbf{Examples} tab for starting points: articles in one, two or three columns, journal papers, theses and books, lab reports, letters, a CV, slides, posters and planners. Each opens in its own window so this tour stays open. The Help tab has the user guide (F1) and every keyboard shortcut.

Happy writing. \textit{The KherveTeX team}

\begin{thebibliography}{99}
\bibitem{example2026} I.~Writer and J.~Typesetter, \emph{Writing documents visually: an example reference}, Example Press, 2026.
\end{thebibliography}
\end{document}

