  \begin{tabular}{rrrr}
    \hline
    \textbf{Frac.} & \textbf{Meas.} & \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Gain} \\ \hline
    0.00 & 0.20 & 0.20 & 1.00 \\
    0.05 & 0.23 & 0.23 & 1.15 \\
    0.10 & 0.27 & 0.26 & 1.35 \\
    0.15 & 0.29 & 0.30 & 1.45 \\
    0.20 & 0.34 & 0.34 & 1.70 \\
    0.30 & 0.44 & 0.44 & 2.20 \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{Filler volume fraction (Frac.), measured and modelled thermal conductivity k (W/(m K)) at 25 °C, and enhancement over the neat resin. Uncertainties: 0.010 to 0.020 W/(m K). The model uses a filler conductivity of 9.7 W/(m K).}
  \label{tab:results}
\end{table}

Minimising Eq. \eqref{eq:chi2} gives $k_f = 9.7\ \mathrm{W\,m^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ with $\chi^2 = 1.4$ for 4 degrees of freedom. The one-standard-deviation interval, 5.7 to 28 W/(m K), is asymmetric because the model saturates when $k_f \gg k_m$. All model values in Table \ref{tab:results} lie within one standard deviation of the measurements.

\chapter{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The fitted filler conductivity is much lower than the in-plane value of several hundred W/(m K) reported for single h-BN crystals \cite{lindqvist2019}. This is expected for randomly oriented platelets separated from the matrix by an interfacial thermal resistance, and it is consistent with other epoxy–ceramic composites \cite{okafor2021,tanaka2023}. Because Eq. \eqref{eq:maxwell} is insensitive to $k_f$ above about 30 W/(m K), the data cannot tell a poor interface from a poor filler; direct interface or single-platelet measurements would be needed.

Two limitations should be kept in mind. The Maxwell model ignores platelet shape and orientation, so it cannot describe the onset of percolation expected above 30 vol\%. And all samples came from one resin batch, so batch-to-batch variation is not included in the uncertainties.

\chapter{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclusions}

Adding 30 vol\% h-BN raises the thermal conductivity of an epoxy resin from 0.20 to 0.44 W/(m K). A one-parameter Maxwell model fits the whole series with an effective filler conductivity of about 10 W/(m K), which suggests that improving the filler–matrix interface, rather than the filler itself, is the most promising route to higher conductivity.

