          "latex": "\\small",
          "type": "InlineRaw"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\itshape",
          "type": "InlineRaw"
        },
        {
          "text": " ² Halden Institute of Applied Physics}",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Author"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Epoxy resins are good electrical insulators but poor conductors of heat, which limits their use in power electronics. We disperse hexagonal boron nitride platelets in a bisphenol-A epoxy at volume fractions from 0 to 30% and measure the thermal conductivity at 25 °C with a transient hot-wire probe. The conductivity rises from 0.20 W/(m K) for the neat resin to 0.44 W/(m K) at 30 vol%, a factor of 2.2. A Maxwell effective-medium model describes all compositions with a single filler conductivity of about 10 W/(m K), far below the bulk value, pointing to interfacial resistance as the limiting factor.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Abstract"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "thermal conductivity",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": " · ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "epoxy composites",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": " · ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "boron nitride",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": " · ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "effective-medium theory",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "type": "Keywords"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Introduction",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Heat removal is a central constraint in compact power electronics, where a polymer encapsulant must insulate electrically yet carry heat away from the device. Neat epoxies conduct only about 0.2 W/(m K), two to three orders of magnitude less than ceramics. The usual remedy is to load the resin with a ceramic filler, and hexagonal boron nitride (h-BN) is attractive because it combines a high in-plane conductivity with electrical resistivity above 10",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": "12",
          "marks": [
            "superscript"
          ],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "text": " Ω cm ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "lindqvist2019",
            "okafor2021"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ".",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Reported gains vary widely between laboratories, partly because the measurement method, the platelet size and the filler–matrix interface differ from study to study. Here we prepare a single series of composites from one resin batch and one filler grade, measure them with one method, and test whether a simple effective-medium model can describe the whole series. Section ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "sec:methods",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " describes the samples and the measurement, Section ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "sec:results",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " gives the results, and Section ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "sec:discussion",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " discusses them.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Materials and methods",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Sample preparation",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Composites were made in five steps:",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "The filler (h-BN platelets, median lateral size 5 µm) was dried for 12 h at 120 °C.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "The resin and hardener were mixed at the stoichiometric ratio and degassed for 10 min.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "The filler was added in portions and homogenised in a planetary mixer at 2000 rpm.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "The mixture was cast into cylindrical moulds 40 mm in diameter and 80 mm tall.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Samples were cured for 2 h at 80 °C and 4 h at 120 °C.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "ListItem"
        }
      ],
      "type": "List"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Six compositions were prepared, from 0 to 30 vol% filler. The densities of the resin and the filler are 1.15 and 2.10 g/cm³, and the density of each composite follows from the rule of mixtures (Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "tab:results",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": ").",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Thermal measurements",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The thermal conductivity was measured at 25 °C with a transient hot-wire probe embedded in each cylinder. A constant heating power ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "q",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " per unit length is applied to the wire and the temperature rise ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\Delta T",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " is recorded against time ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "t",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ". For an ideal line source in an infinite medium the conductivity follows from the slope of ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\Delta T",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " against ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\ln t",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ":",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "\\label{eq:hotwire}\nk = \\frac{q}{4\\pi}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\,\\Delta T}{\\mathrm{d}\\ln t}\\right)^{-1}.",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Each value is the mean of five runs separated by 15 minutes to let the sample relax",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Only the window between 1 s and 10 s was fitted, to avoid wire-contact effects at early times and boundary effects at late times.",
              "marks": [],
              "type": "Text"
            }
          ],
          "type": "Footnote"
        },
        {
          "text": "; the uncertainty is the larger of the run-to-run scatter and the 3% calibration uncertainty of the probe.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 2,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Effective-medium model",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "We compare the data with the Maxwell model for spherical inclusions of conductivity ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "k_f",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " at volume fraction ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\phi",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " in a matrix of conductivity ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "k_m",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ":",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "\\label{eq:maxwell}\nk_{\\mathrm{eff}} = k_m\\,\\frac{k_f + 2k_m + 2\\phi\\,(k_f - k_m)}{k_f + 2k_m - \\phi\\,(k_f - k_m)}.",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "With ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "k_m",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " fixed at the measured value of the neat resin, ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "k_f",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " is the only free parameter. It is found by minimising",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "latex": "\\label{eq:chi2}\n\\chi^2(k_f) = \\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\left(\\frac{k_i - k_{\\mathrm{eff}}(\\phi_i; k_f)}{\\sigma_i}\\right)^2,",
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "MathBlock"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "over the ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "N = 5",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " filled samples, where ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "k_i",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " and ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\sigma_i",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " are the measured conductivity and its uncertainty.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Results",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "tab:results",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " lists the measured conductivities. The neat resin gives ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "k_m = 0.20\\ \\mathrm{W\\,m^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ", and the conductivity grows monotonically with filler content to ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "0.44\\ \\mathrm{W\\,m^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " at 30 vol%.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "rows": [
        [
          "Frac.",
          "Meas.",
          "Model",
          "Gain"
        ],
        [
          "0.00",
          "0.20",
          "0.20",
          "1.00"
        ],
        [
          "0.05",
          "0.23",
          "0.23",
          "1.15"
        ],
        [
          "0.10",
          "0.27",
          "0.26",
          "1.35"
        ],
        [
          "0.15",
          "0.29",
          "0.30",
          "1.45"
        ],
        [
          "0.20",
          "0.34",
          "0.34",
          "1.70"
        ],
        [
          "0.30",
          "0.44",
          "0.44",
          "2.20"
        ]
      ],
      "caption": "Filler volume fraction (Frac.), measured and modelled thermal conductivity k (W/(m K)) at 25 °C, and enhancement over the neat resin. Uncertainties: 0.010 to 0.020 W/(m K). The model uses a filler conductivity of 9.7 W/(m K).",
      "label": "tab:results",
      "alignment": "rrrr",
      "style": "",
      "merges": [],
      "header": true,
      "repeat_header": false,
      "shading": {},
      "type": "Table"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Minimising Eq. ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "eq:chi2",
          "kind": "eqref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " gives ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "k_f = 9.7\\ \\mathrm{W\\,m^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " with ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "\\chi^2 = 1.4",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " for 4 degrees of freedom. The one-standard-deviation interval, 5.7 to 28 W/(m K), is asymmetric because the model saturates when ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "k_f \\gg k_m",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": ". All model values in Table ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "tab:results",
          "kind": "ref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " lie within one standard deviation of the measurements.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Discussion",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The fitted filler conductivity is much lower than the in-plane value of several hundred W/(m K) reported for single h-BN crystals ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "lindqvist2019"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ". This is expected for randomly oriented platelets separated from the matrix by an interfacial thermal resistance, and it is consistent with other epoxy–ceramic composites ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "keys": [
            "okafor2021",
            "tanaka2023"
          ],
          "style": "cite",
          "type": "Citation"
        },
        {
          "text": ". Because Eq. ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "label": "eq:maxwell",
          "kind": "eqref",
          "type": "CrossRef"
        },
        {
          "text": " is insensitive to ",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        },
        {
          "latex": "k_f",
          "type": "MathInline"
        },
        {
          "text": " above about 30 W/(m K), the data cannot tell a poor interface from a poor filler; direct interface or single-platelet measurements would be needed.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Two limitations should be kept in mind. The Maxwell model ignores platelet shape and orientation, so it cannot describe the onset of percolation expected above 30 vol%. And all samples came from one resin batch, so batch-to-batch variation is not included in the uncertainties.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Conclusions",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": true,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Adding 30 vol% h-BN raises the thermal conductivity of an epoxy resin from 0.20 to 0.44 W/(m K). A one-parameter Maxwell model fits the whole series with an effective filler conductivity of about 10 W/(m K), which suggests that improving the filler–matrix interface, rather than the filler itself, is the most promising route to higher conductivity.",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "alignment": "justify",
      "type": "Paragraph"
    },
    {
      "level": 1,
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Acknowledgements",
          "marks": [],
          "type": "Text"
        }
      ],
      "numbered": false,
      "label": null,
      "type": "Section"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "This work was supported by the Example Foundation (grant EF-0000). We thank the technical staff of the Northfield materials workshop for help with the moulds, and the maintainers of ",
